  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component implements the \"happy path\" of a live region correctly, but contains a critical failure in semantic association and a major gap in focus management. While it passes the \"automated\" feel of accessibility (ARIA attributes are present), it creates a fragmented experience for keyboard and screen reader users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \n- *Predicted*: Focus management gaps after search. (Confirmed: Focus remains on the trigger, forcing keyboard users to tab through the form again to reach results).\n- *Predicted*: Redundant labeling. (Confirmed: Both `<label>` and `aria-label` are used).\n- *Predicted*: Semantic hiding issues. (Confirmed: `display: none` used on the label).\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n**1. Label removed from Accessibility Tree via CSS**\n- **Evidence**: `CSS: line 14` (`.search-container label { display: none; }`) and `JSX: line 31` (`<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>`).\n- **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG Citation**: 1.3.1 Info and Relationships / 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions.\n- **Why this matters**: `display: none` removes the element from the DOM's accessibility tree entirely. Even though `htmlFor` is present in the JSX, the browser ignores the label. The input is effectively unlabeled unless the user relies on the `aria-label`, but the design intent was to have a semantic label.\n- **Fix**: Replace `display: none` with the `.sr-only` class already defined in the CSS. This hides the label visually but keeps it available to assistive technology.\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n**1. Missing Focus Management for Search Results**\n- **Evidence**: `JSX: lines 54-68` (Results rendering logic).\n- **User Group**: Keyboard-only and Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG Citation**: 2.4.3 Focus Order.\n- **Why this matters**: When a user submits the search, the results are injected below the form. Focus remains on the \"Search\" button. A keyboard user must tab through the form again or tab through the rest of the page to reach the results. For a screen reader user, while the live region announces \"Found X results,\" there is no programmatic way to jump directly to the start of those results.\n- **Fix**: \n    - Add a `ref` to the `<h2>Search Results</h2>` element.\n    - Set `tabIndex=\"-1\"` on that heading.\n    - Use a `useEffect` or the `handleSearch` callback to move focus to the heading once `isLoading` becomes false and `results.length > 0`.\n\n**2. Redundant Accessible Naming**\n- **Evidence**: `JSX: line 34` (`aria-label=\"Search query\"`) and `JSX: line 31` (`<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>`).\n- **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG Citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n- **Why this matters**: Providing both a label and an `aria-label` can cause some screen readers to announce the name twice or prioritize the `aria-label` and ignore the semantic label. It creates \"chatter\" and inconsistency.\n- **Fix**: Remove `aria-label=\"Search query\"` from the input. Rely on the semantic `<label>` (once the CSS `display: none` is fixed).\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n- **`aria-busy` State**: The results container (`.results-container`) should be marked with `aria-busy=\"true\"` while `isLoading` is true. This tells assistive technology that the region is currently updating and to wait before announcing changes.\n- **Search Result Links**: Currently, the results are just `<h3>` and `<p>`. In a real-world scenario, these should be wrapped in `<a>` tags. If they are links, the `<h3>` should be inside the `<a>` to provide a clear accessible name for the link.\n- **Empty State Focus**: When \"No results found\" is displayed, focus should ideally move to that message so the user doesn't have to hunt for the failure notification.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | `display: none` on label + redundant naming |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | No focus jump to results |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Reliance on `sr-only` for critical labels |\n| Cognitive | **LOW** | Interaction model is simple and consistent |\n| Vestibular | **LOW** | No motion/animation present |\n| Auditory | **LOW** | No audio content |\n| Env. Contrast | **LOW** | Blue/White contrast appears sufficient |\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Will hear the live region announce \"Searching...\" and \"Found 3 results,\" but will be confused why the input field seems to have no label (due to `display: none`) or will hear the name twice.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Will experience a \"dead end\" after clicking Search. They are left on the button and must manually navigate down to the results, which is inefficient for large result sets.\n- **Low vision user**: If using a screen magnifier, the lack of focus movement to the results means they may not even realize the results have appeared below the fold.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REVISE**\n\nThe component is \"technically\" compliant with automated live-region checks, but it fails the real-world usability test for keyboard and screen reader users. The `display: none` on the label is a critical semantic error. The lack of focus management is a major usability barrier. \n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT:**\n1. Fix the label visibility using `.sr-only` instead of `display: none`.\n2. Implement programmatic focus movement to the results heading upon search completion.\n3. Remove redundant `aria-label`.\n4. Add `aria-busy` to the results region during loading.",
